A||N||6||Tabs||2M4 Take-offs||||1||
Z||000000||Tabs||08-08-2001||05:04 PM||||Is it just me or do auto 2M4's have a bad take-off??<P>I mean last night I raced a Z24 and the only reason I lost was cuz off my bad take-off.<P>I've notice that after when the car shifts itself in2 2nd it really picks up.<P>Does anybody else with a auto 2M4 have the same problem???<P>Tabs<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||88CoupeV6||08-08-2001||05:22 PM||||I don't think Jaygee79's is all that bad, but a standard would be a lot quicker.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroLisa||08-08-2001||05:24 PM||||I've never driven an auto 2M4, but I had a 5 speed 86 2M4... It ran 0-60 in 10.6, not bad for being 14 yrs old... but anyway, moral of the story is, that when I raced Jonathan McCreery's 86 GT, on takeoff I was right with him. Granted, he kicked my butt, but it was close for the first couple seconds  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||baldlobo||08-08-2001||07:31 PM||||you rev it up to about 3800rpm, that should help your take off.||||reg||1
Z||000004||kslish||08-09-2001||12:37 AM||||Next race hit the gas a little while on the brakes to load up the torque converter.  That should get you off the line a little quicker.  <P>You can also advance the ignition timing a little bit for better throttle response and a little more power....but then you can't use anything except premium gas or it'll ping.  I don't mind spending the extra change for premium in mine because it still seems cheap to fill up in comparison to my GT (31 mpg on the highway in my automatic coupe).  You can only do this trick on '84-'86 distributor equipped Dukes, because of the DIS setup on '87-'88 4cyl Fieros.  <P>There are some other small tricks that I'm not going to give away (at least not yet) because if I blow up my engine, I can only blame myself.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   Some it also isn't cheap.  I just got sick of trying to keep up with 6 cyl Fieros on back road dashes so I decided to make a few "changes" to be a little more competitive. <P>The only other auto 4cyl Fiero I've driven was Fierokid87's coupe.  I know mine is a lot quicker than his was, which is probably a small part of the reason he ripped out his 4cyl and is in the middle of putting in a 6.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Lisa, you're welcome to borrow my keys the next time I see you.  I need an impartial person to give me an opinion anyways on how it drives.  Of course, I've seen how you can flog your own car so maybe that's not such a bright idea on my part...hehe.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||x-thumpr-x||08-09-2001||11:36 AM||||Have an 84 2M4 myself. I find I get a quiker start off if I rev to 2500RPM before I let the clutch out. Mind you it's easier to do this in a manual then an automatic.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fotofrank||08-09-2001||01:25 PM||||I've got an '84 Duke. Put the car in 1st and start out that way. I wind it to about 32mph then shift into 2nd(manually) shifting to drive at about 40 or so. Seems ok to me but you be the judge. Getting off the line has a lot to do with the engine HP and torque as well as how you drive. Don't stomp on the gas just accellerate smoothly from dead stop to go fast. Good luck. <P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||6
